{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 120 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b8f9bc2ac80fb4c7b0854c9e3a0bc3489c171fa4fa53964549f26532c2b3c05f" :edb/db-before-t 119 :edb/db-after-t 120 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533432 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:50.080Z" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357491 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357495 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357456 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357457 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357659 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357661 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357679 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357681 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186059874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357694 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186060448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059280 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186060448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357037 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186060448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186060448 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0, 1)$, compute $P(0.70 < Z < 0.92)$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7580$ | $0.7611$ | $0.7642$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7881$ | $0.7910$ | $0.7939$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8159$ | $0.8186$ | $0.8212$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186060448 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(0.70<Z<0.92).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186060448 1074 "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(0.70<Z<0.92).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3eb46852c53e4fa13aaa29c31623153bfef604d138f15c6da67732d5730b785f.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.70<Z<0.92) & =P(Z<0.92)-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.92)-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =Φ(0.92)-Φ(0.70)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.70) & =0.7580 \\\\ Φ(0.92) & =0.8212\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<Z<0.92) & =Φ(0.92)-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =0.8212-0.7580 \\\\  & =0.0632.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357654 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357483 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357473 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357474 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357475 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357476 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357477 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357478 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357479 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357502 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357504 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357503 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186078595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357505 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357749 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357750 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357751 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357389 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357390 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357391 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357374 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357375 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357376 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357377 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357378 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357379 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083322 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357380 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357369 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357370 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357371 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357372 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357373 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357381 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357382 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357383 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357384 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357385 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357386 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357387 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357758 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357752 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357753 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082679 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357315 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083963 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by two machines, $A$ and $B$. In a particular random sample, $37$ of the components sampled were produced by machine $A$, and $43$ were produced by machine $B$. On inspecting the sampled components, it was found that $5$ defective components were produced by machine $A$ and $3$ defective components were produced by machine $B$. What is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083963 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by two machines, $A$ and $B.$ In a particular random sample, $37$ of the components sampled were produced by machine $A,$ and $43$ were produced by machine $B.$ On inspecting the sampled components, it was found that $5$ defective components were produced by machine $A$ and $3$ defective components were produced by machine $B.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083963 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen component was produced by machine $A$, and let $D$ be the event that the chosen component is defective. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(D\\cap A),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $A,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{80}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(D\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $B,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{80}+\\frac{3}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082691 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357077 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083965 1071 "Sophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B$. She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A$, and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B$. Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083965 1071 "Sophia has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ She receives $35%$ of her emails in account $A,$ and the remaining $65%$ go into account $B.$ Approximately $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email is spam?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083965 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $65%$ in account $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $15%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam while $25%$ of the emails received in account $B$ are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.15,\\,P(S|{A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.15)(0.35)+(0.25)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.215.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082697 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356959 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083966 1071 "A math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B$. Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$, and $65%$ are in Group $B$. If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265$, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186083966 1071 "A math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A,$ and $65%$ are in Group $B.$ If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186083966 1074 "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$ and $65%$ are in Group $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top  grade is $0.265,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.265.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.265 & =(0.20)(0.35)+P(G|{A}^{′})(0.65) \\\\ 0.265 & =0.07+0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.195 \\\\ P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade is $0.30.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357394 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357497 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357498 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357499 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357500 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357501 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357484 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357489 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357490 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087784 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357488 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357579 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357580 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357581 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357584 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357585 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357392 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357395 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357396 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357397 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087875 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357560 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357561 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357562 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357563 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357564 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357565 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357566 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357586 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357557 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357558 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357559 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357574 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357575 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357583 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357714 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357736 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357737 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357466 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357467 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357468 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357469 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357470 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186087894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357465 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087353 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186088455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356965 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088455 1071 "![](images/90/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088455 1071 "![](images/90/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sqrt{2}\\sec t, y = 2\\tan t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186088455 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b4/b43dcf6c343994e3226e2840a0ae80f60484c8de5d7984f61ec6447185181a76.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087365 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186088457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357053 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088457 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186088457 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 4e^{3x}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186088457 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-2(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-2-6x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357527 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357528 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357529 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357530 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357531 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357532 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357533 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092232 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357534 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357548 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357550 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357540 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357555 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357556 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357571 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357573 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357582 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357590 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357364 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357367 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091534 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357185 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092894 1071 "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}\\cos x = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092894 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092894 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091582 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357235 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092902 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram $P^{′}$ under the action of $T$. The parallelogram $P^{′}$ has vertices $(0, 2), (-3, 3), (12, 4)$, and $(7, 3)$. Which of the following are vertices of $P$?\n\n1. $(-2, 2)$\n2. $(1,-1)$\n3. $(4, 4)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092902 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,2),(-3,3),(12,4),$ and $(7,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,-1)$\n- $(4,4)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092902 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,2),(-3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091594 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356932 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092904 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092904 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092904 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|1\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =|-2-0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091600 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357339 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092905 1071 "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1, 0), (0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)$. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T$, given below?\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092905 1071 "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092905 1074 "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=7\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-7|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091612 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357324 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092907 1071 "![](images/4f/4f31b9f180b1b766451685428fc289766832fe5d0c7d8902a601ebf060eab0e7.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T$, given below?\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092907 1071 "![](images/4f/4f31b9f180b1b766451685428fc289766832fe5d0c7d8902a601ebf060eab0e7.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092907 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 0=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-10|\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\  & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091642 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357147 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092912 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092912 1071 "Given that $λ =-3$ and $λ = 4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092912 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+14{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 14 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091696 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356944 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092921 1071 "$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2})=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186092921 1071 "What is $\\sinh (\\ln \\sqrt{2})$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186092921 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/\\sqrt{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2-1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186101574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100575 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186101574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357355 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186101574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186101574 1071 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\sin x$ about $x = 0$ is" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186101574 1071 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=0$ is" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186101574 1074 "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =0+1\\cdot x+0\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186105823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357393 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186105831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357400 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186105833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357398 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186105833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357399 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186110658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357451 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186110659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357448 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186110659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357449 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186110664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357450 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186110251 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186111426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357134 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111426 1074 "Consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $x-4y=-8.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{4}+2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{x}{4}+2,\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx-4y\\ge -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y>2x \\\\ x-4y\\ge -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186110257 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186111427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356976 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111427 1071 "What is the graph of $y = \\arccos x$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186111427 1071 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186111427 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its $y$-intercept is $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357544 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357545 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357546 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357547 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115210 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357539 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115210 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357541 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115210 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357542 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115210 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357543 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357536 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357537 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186115211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357538 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357520 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357521 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357509 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357514 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357518 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357519 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357709 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357732 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186119884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357735 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119395 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186120552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357021 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120552 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120552 1071 "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{14}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186120552 1074 "Let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119437 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186120559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357350 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120559 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2, 25)$, compute $P(-0.65 < X < - 0.25)$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-0.5$ | $0.2981$ | $0.2946$ | $0.2912$ |\n| $-0.4$ | $0.3336$ | $0.3300$ | $0.3264$ |\n| $-0.3$ | $0.3707$ | $0.3669$ | $0.3632$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186120559 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2,25),$ compute $P(-0.65<X<-0.25).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186120559 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =2$ and $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.65<X<-0.25) & =P(\\frac{-0.65-2}{5}<Z<\\frac{-0.25-2}{5}) \\\\  & =P(-0.53<Z<-0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d73cc4661eb9f68f636803918b3e55942e6538e4e70d13277d125386dad257e.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<-0.45) & =P(Z<-0.45)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.45)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.53) & =0.2981 \\\\ Φ(-0.45) & =0.3264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<-0.45) & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-0.53) \\\\  & =0.3264-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.0283.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-0.65<X<-0.25)=0.0283.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186124494 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357744 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186124569 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357454 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186124569 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357455 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357754 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186124592 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357755 13194139533432 175