c equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157972 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{25}=5$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156777 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356947 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157981 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{1-x}>4,\\,x>2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157981 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\frac{3x + 2}{1 - x} > 4, x > 2$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157981 1074 "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ -x & <-2 \\\\ 1-x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-x$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-x,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & >4 \\\\ (1-x)\\cdot \\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & <(1-x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 3x+2 & <4-4x \\\\ 7x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there are no numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x<\\frac{2}{7}.$ So, the inequality has no solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156789 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357100 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157983 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $y^{′} - y = x^{3} + 3x^{2}$. Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(-1,-2)$\n2. $(1, 1)$\n3. $(1,-2)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157983 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157983 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156795 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357207 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157984 1071 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2y + x^{2}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}$ at $P$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157984 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157984 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+2{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,-1)} & ={(4y+2{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =4(-1)+2(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156801 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356934 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157985 1071 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation $y^{′} = y^{2} + x^{2}$. Given that the point $(1, 1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of $\\left.y^{″}\\right|_{(1, 1)}$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157985 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157985 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =({y}^{2})^{′}+({x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $({y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}+2(1)(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156807 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357280 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157986 1071 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x^{3} - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(1, 2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n3. $(1, 2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157986 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157986 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156825 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357070 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157989 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157989 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$(2x - 3)/(\\ln (x - 2)) \\ge 0, x > 3$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157989 1074 "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156831 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356918 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157990 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x}\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157990 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$4xe^{2x} - 5e^{2x} \\le 0$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157990 1074 "First, we factor the given inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}(4x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{2x}(4x-5)}{{e}^{2x}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ 4x-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156867 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357095 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157996 1071 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = ye^{x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $(0, e)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(0, e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(0, e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(0, e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186157996 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186157996 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156903 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186158002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357064 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158002 1071 "![](images/60/600981b59a97fd1d63c9da1f7ef45d58c8a2d5dcad1ec05df91a380e0f4e154e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158002 1071 "![](images/60/600981b59a97fd1d63c9da1f7ef45d58c8a2d5dcad1ec05df91a380e0f4e154e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 2\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186158002 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/74/747613184fe04ecd53fb69e6e00c5d91152e571e9bc4db81adeef1063c057491.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156909 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186158003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356963 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158003 1071 "![](images/7b/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186158003 1071 "![](images/7b/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 3\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186158003 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/eb/ebb0f765fca0bc4e3a0a789800779a51eb4173cf0384b8b3628ac679a5a3d692.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the 