 [17592186162001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357678 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357682 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357683 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160730 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357051 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162578 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162578 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_{i}:=C_{i} + kC_{2}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162578 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=5$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{2}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160814 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357099 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162592 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162592 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162592 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160844 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357024 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162597 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{1}{y}_{3}+{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162597 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x, y) = x_{1}y_{1} - 3x_{1}y_{3} + x_{2}y_{3} - x_{3}y_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162597 1074 "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{1}{y}_{3}+{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-3){x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160850 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357259 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162598 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $R^{2}$?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162598 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162598 1074 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160856 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357285 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162599 1071 "Consider the quadratic form\n$Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 3x_{1}x_{3} - x_{2}x_{3} + x_{3}^{2}$.\nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162599 1071 "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162599 1074 "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-4)+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+0+16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160868 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356987 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162601 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162601 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form $x^{T}Ax$ in terms of the components of $x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162601 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160886 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357042 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162604 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162604 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\cdot 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162604 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160910 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357281 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162608 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162608 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, express the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162608 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{2}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})^{2}+3({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})(-{y}_{2})+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160982 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357312 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162620 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162620 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form $x^{T}Ay$ in terms of the components of $x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162620 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(3){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-3){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+-4{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ -3{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-3{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160988 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357133 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162621 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162621 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & -2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x, y) = x_{1}y_{1} - 2x_{1}y_{2} + 2x_{2}y_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162621 1074 "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+2=3.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161024 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357338 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162627 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162627 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, express the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} - 2x_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162627 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving these for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2}){y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161030 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357072 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162628 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162628 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form $x^{T}Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162628 1074 "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161036 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357034 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162629 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162629 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ ∗ & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ∗ & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\cdot 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162629 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161048 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357255 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162631 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162631 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D = P^{T}AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P$?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162631 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are orthogonal. Finally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161078 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357291 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162636 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x) =-x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 2x_{2}^{2}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162636 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162636 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161084 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356967 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162637 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} - x_{3}^{2} - 6x_{1}x_{2}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162637 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162637 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161102 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357225 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162640 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162640 1071 "Consider the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n1. $R(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{1}x_{2}$\n2. $S(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n3. $T(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2}$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162640 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161120 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356937 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162643 1071 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $λ_{1} = 7$ and $λ_{2} = 10$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is indefinite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162643 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162643 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161126 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357341 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162644 1071 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 9$ and $det(A) = 18$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162644 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162644 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161132 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356974 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162645 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186162645 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-1$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186162645 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186167331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165165 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186167331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357091 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186167331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186167331 1071 "Let $f(x, y) = x^{2} - 4y$, where $x = 2st$ and $y = s^{2}t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186167331 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186167331 1074 "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186171967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357368 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172027 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357365 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172027 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357366 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357549 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357622 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357623 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357626 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172031 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357627 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357642 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172032 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357643 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357636 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357637 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171285 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186172615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357138 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172615 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{5x + 12} - 3$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186172615 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186172615 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{5x+12} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{5x+12})^{3} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ 5x+12 & =27 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186175965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357388 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186179680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357493 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186179680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357494 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186179740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357492 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186179824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357535 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186188989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187980 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186188989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356914 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186188989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186188989 1071 "![](images/2e/2edd37e6318a7d78b3e6f496bb3f688d006949ccab983f75cece9993817f6b69.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186188989 1071 "![](images/2e/2edd37e6318a7d78b3e6f496bb3f688d006949ccab983f75cece9993817f6b69.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\cos θ$, shown above." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186188989 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186221434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357414 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186222050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220954 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186222050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356975 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186222050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186222050 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186222050 1071 "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t} + 2te^{2t}$ for $s > 2$." 13194139533432 17592186045585 false] [17592186222050 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186229175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357411 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186231433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357707 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186231433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357708 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186354663 1081 "\\frac{{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}+9}" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186354663 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [1759