$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-{s}^{2}}-s\\cdot (0)-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}}{s(1-s)}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{1-s}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1-s}{1-s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356994 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1069 "q-89465" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $L{{f}^{″}(t)}$ for $s>1,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ $f(0)=1,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{{s}^{3}-{s}^{2}},\\,s>1.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1072 #uuid "964e6ef7-c836-5063-a9c4-a9017f00d793" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357409 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{{s}^{3}-{s}^{2}}-s\\cdot (1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-s+1)}{{s}^{2}(s-1)}-s \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1}{s-1}-s \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-s+1-{s}^{2}+s}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357151 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357410 1069 "q-89472" 13