text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)={\\begin{aligned}0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t<1 \\\\ -7\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t>3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this definite integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{s}{e}^{-st}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s}).\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356962 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1069 "q-89562" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1072 #uuid "f5f42eae-66ad-5bef-a8dd-b0f6039d3139" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357412 1074 "Note that the factor $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {cos}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,4\\le 3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4\\le 7.\n$$\n\nSince $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} & \\le \\frac{0}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} \\\\ x-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357173 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1069 "q-89572" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x+3)\\ge 0,\\,x<-1.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1072 #uuid "15a6c04e-80c7-5ce2-9525-875efbc8c036" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357413 1074 "Since $x<-1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-1 \\\\ 2x & <-2 \\\\ 2x-1 & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2x-1$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<-1).$ So, we can divide both sides by $2x-1,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-1)(x+3) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{2x-1} & \\le \\frac{0}{2x-1} \\\\ x+3 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\le -3$ and $x<-1.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\le -3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357092 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1069 "q-89595" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1072 #uuid "1bbc9aab-1102-5ce0-b189-5bc4cc63a0ee" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357414 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =L{3}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({s}^{2}+9)-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+27-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357269 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1069 "q-89596" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ for $s>0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1072 #uuid "10056c0c-db46-5f71-a7c7-edd694fbf4d4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357415 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357076 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1069 "q-89651" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2}\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1072 #uuid "c30e5682-d2e0-5625-9166-382c2b2b89c5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357416 1074 "We know that the denominator ${x}^{4}+2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${x}^{4}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+3x & \\le 2{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ 3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356978 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1069 "q-89660" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1072 #uuid "e48e97fe-2f87-5b00-958b-28a6ef014415" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357417 1074 "Since ${4}^{x}+{x}^{2}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\frac{0}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357125 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1069 "q-89661" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6{x}^{5}+18x>3x.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1072 #uuid "a029723c-ae65-5f0f-afa0-5d78d651bfe2" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357418 1074 "First, we move all terms to the left-hand side and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}+18x & >3x \\\\ 6{x}^{5}+15x & >0 \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{4}+5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{x}^{4}+5$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{4}+5)}{2{x}^{4}+5} & >\\frac{0}{2{x}^{4}+5} \\\\ 3x & >0 \\\\ x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357236 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1069 "q-89671" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}>\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1072 #uuid "485c1961-1e82-5f5e-90bd-bbc3bcf6a36e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357419 1074 "Note that the factor $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {sin}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,-3\\le 2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3\\le -1.\n$$\n\nSince $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & >\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ (2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & <(2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ x+2 & <4-3x \\\\ 4x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356940 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1069 "q-89676" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)<0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1072 #uuid "f2bee22d-7ec5-5d27-a0a4-8fc204928c70" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357420 1074 "Since $-3{x}^{2}-4$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)}{-3{x}^{2}-4} & >\\frac{0}{-3{x}^{2}-4} \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357353 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1069 "q-89768" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1071 "![](images/43/432f8963425031983284db54310c8ae604bc993de66494dceb48e6a4e2bcdf73.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1072 #uuid "62be1dd8-deea-56ad-b15b-be7e2193c59e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357421 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-7)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357135 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1069 "q-89774" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1071 "![](images/24/245037d572c2bc9c81d3cdfb7a485b167c15223c68e3dbb8e40eaa2cc7650da9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm x.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1072 #uuid "025e8394-70bf-5ddb-a31e-c258335fd7a4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357422 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357220 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1069 "q-89778" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1072 #uuid "7a2f9af9-e452-5059-98bf-392c4d9486d5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357423 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356952 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1069 "q-89811" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1072 #uuid "e15a1e41-b15f-5405-8fc5-9c68aefa2e00" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357424 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{4}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{4}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{u}_{4}(t)}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}} \\\\ {u}_{4}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}} & ={u}_{4}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357357 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1069 "q-89816" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1071 "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{2s+15}{{s}^{2}+9}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1072 #uuid "64b4c22e-e778-50dc-9fe8-6d89421eb4b3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357425 1074 "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2s+15}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{2s+15}{{s}^{2}+9}$ is $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357308 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1069 "q-89818" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1071 "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}+{e}^{-5s}}{s}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1072 #uuid "cbe99861-2548-540a-b8b9-96c85115c5cd" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357426 1074 "We decompose the fraction into a sum of fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}+{e}^{-5s}}{s}=\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{8}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{u}_{5}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{5}(t)=u(t-5)$ and ${u}_{8}(t)=u(t-8)$ are the unit step functions.\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}} \\\\  & =2{u}_{8}(t)+{u}_{5}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}+{e}^{-5s}}{s}$ is $f(t)=2{u}_{8}(t)+{u}_{5}(t).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357274 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1069 "q-89820" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1071 "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{8{s}^{3}-24}{{s}^{5}}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1072 #uuid "92954c9d-fb0d-5475-b7bd-8bc640ee1e00" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357427 1074 "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8{s}^{3}-24}{{s}^{5}}=\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0, \\\\ L{{t}^{4}} & =\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}} & =8{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}} \\\\  & =8t-{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{8{s}^{3}-24}{{s}^{5}}$ is $f(t)=8t-{t}^{4}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357066 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1069 "q-89824" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1072 #uuid "8a04da01-cc3d-55e1-98fa-3ff454ac0b60" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357428 1074 "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)(s+3)}=\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{6(s-3)}-\\frac{1}{6(s+3)}} & =\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-3}}-\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}-9}$ is $f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3t}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357089 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1069 "q-89826" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1071 "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+5s}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1072 #uuid "76b3c8da-0b16-54ad-be3e-4282f002aa33" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357429 1074 "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+5s}=\\frac{5}{s(s+5)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s+5}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5},\\,s>-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s+5}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+5}} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+5s}$ is $f(t)=1-{e}^{-5t}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357096 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1069 "q-89866" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y-4x}{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,5)$\n- $(-2,-4)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1072 #uuid "ebe79c85-c57b-513c-b661-1636edd13781" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357430 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,5)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(1,5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(5)-4(1)}{1} \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-4)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-2,-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-4)-4(-2)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-3)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-1,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-3)-4(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357342 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1069 "q-89869" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1072 #uuid "3a6c55b2-0dbc-5417-9666-4eed70a77fd5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357431 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357190 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1069 "q-89871" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1072 #uuid "e87b95d9-4b1d-5eb0-8c89-8d155a899eb3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357432 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357276 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1069 "q-89872" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1072 #uuid "b92d6550-a190-5c3e-8b25-14493984cf60" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357433 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357258 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1069 "q-89876" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1072 #uuid "a60ded1d-9b7a-5230-91d0-5142a988b744" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357434 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =(y+2x)+2 \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y+2x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(y+2x+2)|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =-1+2(0)+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357168 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1069 "q-89877" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(-1,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1072 #uuid "08343168-f5b9-5cbf-8e26-551cf88e29da" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357435 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =(y+{e}^{x})+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,0)} & ={(y+2{e}^{x})|}_{(-1,0)} \\\\  & =0+2{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357036 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1069 "q-89878" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1072 #uuid "d7deff3b-3993-5488-9f6d-2217cb77be44" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357436 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =9(1)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357206 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1069 "q-89879" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1072 #uuid "1a7c1f39-61e7-5e48-ab62-48e1a7ca7f8d" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357437 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356925 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1069 "q-89880" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Given that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1072 #uuid "e73c3acf-5e51-585b-b789-b2885da3b497" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357438 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{(y)^{′}{x}^{2}-y({x}^{2})^{′}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}-2xy)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)} & ={\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-2(1))}{(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357068 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1069 "q-89882" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1072 #uuid "c7ecc714-ee9c-5f26-8607-228e2847ffed" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357439 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\  & =-1+0+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357209 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1069 "q-89884" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}={e}^{y}+x.$ Given that the point $(-1,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(-1,0)}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1072 #uuid "46eab164-da55-5474-bc1b-3dab4766d4ea" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357440 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={({e}^{y}+x)}^{′} \\\\ ({y}^{′})^{′} & ={({e}^{y})}^{′}+{(x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={y}^{′}{e}^{y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${({e}^{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & ={y}^{′}{e}^{y}+1 \\\\ {y}^{″} & =({e}^{y}+x)\\cdot {e}^{y}+1 \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={e}^{2y}+x{e}^{y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(-1,0)} & ={({e}^{2y}+x{e}^{y}+1)|}_{(-1,0)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(0)}+(-1){e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357360 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1069 "q-89885" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1072 #uuid "72dda5b9-2988-5be6-b882-d73d509880d4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357441 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357009 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1069 "q-89886" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1072 #uuid "21b379b1-3944-5c72-96df-e324e7834ad0" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357442 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\  & =(y-x)-1 \\\\  & =y-x-1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x-1)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357086 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1069 "q-89887" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1072 #uuid "1e244cae-49ac-5727-b109-5857ab506f84" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357443 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357195 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1069 "q-89888" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1072 #uuid "0272b49a-4d96-50b3-ac67-e22a2911e738" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357444 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357359 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1069 "q-89889" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1072 #uuid "5d8694a2-ea00-53e6-abdb-fa32dda7bdcf" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357445 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357343 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1069 "q-89894" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1072 #uuid "7cbbdaa7-7656-5f0c-adc2-8162521aa896" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357446 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4xy)|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(0)(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)\\cdot y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =4y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4y+4x(4xy) \\\\  & =4y(1+4{x}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4y(1+4{x}^{2}))|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(1)(1+4(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(0,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357300 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1069 "q-89895" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,-3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1072 #uuid "e18f8cd9-b1f2-5b99-9f93-cbb0a6aa2a95" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357447 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,-3),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{x}{y})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(-3)} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-x\\cdot \\frac{x}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{2}-{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,-3).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357243 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1069 "q-90053" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1071 "![](images/4b/4b3487492c235ddeaa46d6b64cc3cc908e02bacf061ab84474401c9d6e3ce634.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1072 #uuid "a92e14eb-f173-59ac-b03c-a89b1aac62a6" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357448 1074 "Consider the line $x+2y=2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x+1,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+2y\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le 2x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge 2 \\\\ y\\le 2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357212 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1069 "q-90056" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1071 "![](images/2b/2b2f637d9468aa104dff160b1ebf1f764868e14704adc02e870ea19fa12e4b17.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1072 #uuid "0fd70c9c-088d-5ef8-91a5-be22db897083" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357449 1074 "Consider the line $x+3y=6.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies  *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" or equal to inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{3}x+2,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+3y\\le 6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2-x.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le 2-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y\\le 6 \\\\ y\\le 2-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357017 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1069 "q-90319" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1071 "![](images/38/387709ca72b5591528b5f5ac95886f0dab331ef2cb67955803e7a252cecef966.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1072 #uuid "d7a91a0e-5211-5376-84ce-57c0c1ab08fa" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357450 1074 "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge \\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x+3.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le 2x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines our shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge \\frac{1}{2}x \\\\ y\\le 2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356970 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1069 "q-90328" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1071 "![](images/79/79222482da6f597532f514fe67facb3e9eefddbb7c1bf491dd3eac480e0886e2.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities represents the shaded region shown in the graph above?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1072 #uuid "c068aace-32d1-5274-8338-e6defa46e410" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357451 1074 "Consider the line $y=2x-4.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2x-4.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 2x-4 \\\\ y<-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357001 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1069 "q-90485" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1)\\ge 0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1072 #uuid "477fb1bb-4cfe-5309-9a32-c387bdafb083" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357452 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are  $x=-1,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357048 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1069 "q-90487" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1071 "Solve the inequality $(1-x)^{2}(x-3)(x+1)>0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1072 #uuid "acae2fad-7394-5d93-abd4-412c79ab05d4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357453 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-x)^{2}(x-3)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-1,$ $x=1$  and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(1,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(1-x)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357288 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1069 "q-90510" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then what equation must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1072 #uuid "24da67c3-c09f-5344-b59d-ad6ac56f642b" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357454 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357256 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1069 "q-90524" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1072 #uuid "5549d2e5-dd18-5f16-b684-aafa642ce0fc" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357455 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+3u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-3{u}^{′}t-9u+3u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+3{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{″}t+3{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357040 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1069 "q-90536" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1071 "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1072 #uuid "4b9462f0-7c1a-53bf-b48b-ceebd886cef2" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357456 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356928 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1069 "q-90541" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1071 "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1072 #uuid "4fc5baf3-102e-5555-96a9-a277910bcc59" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357457 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357294 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1069 "q-90572" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1071 "Is the function $g(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+5{z}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1072 #uuid "c9681d32-6495-5140-80f9-556018759b3e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357458 1074 "A function $g(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}g(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)^{2}+(tx)(ty)^{2}+5(tz)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{y}^{2}+5{t}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}g(x,y,z)={t}^{n}(2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+5{z}^{2})\n$$\n\nand so $g(x,y,z)$ is not homogeneous." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357240 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1069 "q-90573" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1071 "Is the function $h(x,y,z)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}-8{z}^{3}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1072 #uuid "9f101c8c-f88e-58b7-901d-7acf86c2b17c" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357459 1074 "A function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}h(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{3}-8(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}{x}^{3}y+{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}-8{t}^{3}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}h(x,y,z)={t}^{n}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}-8{z}^{3})\n$$\n\nand so $h(x,y,z)$ is not homogeneous." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356968 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1069 "q-90582" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(y)={y}^{2}+y$\n- $g(x,y)={x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}$\n- $h(x,y,z)=xyz$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1072 #uuid "9a2bd93d-e29f-5907-89bd-df4c7adaca45" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357460 1074 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$  is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2.$ However, it also contains a term ($y$) that is of degree $1.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $2.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4.$ It is a polynomial of degree $4,$ and all of its terms are of degree $4.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ with no terms of degree other than $3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357352 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1069 "q-90588" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1071 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1072 #uuid "b71bf807-bd33-573c-8119-59dd5615c806" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357461 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-2=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{4}}{2(tx)(ty)-6(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}{x}^{4}}{2{t}^{2}xy-6{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}({x}^{4})}{{t}^{2}(2xy-6{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{2}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357345 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1069 "q-90589" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1071 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x}\\,?\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1072 #uuid "42fbff11-4e84-55d4-844f-cc76337f9e47" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357462 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-1=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}}{4(ty)-(tx)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}}{4ty-tx} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3})}{t(4y-x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{3}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357142 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1069 "q-90594" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1072 #uuid "97a10503-5999-522a-bdc2-90aed9e63d0f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357463 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357028 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1069 "q-90596" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1072 #uuid "cdfca633-f287-5217-90a1-9967e19b9571" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357464 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356957 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1069 "q-90597" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1071 "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1072 #uuid "dc83a0da-147c-536d-ae44-6851708bb02f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357465 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357246 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1069 "q-90601" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}).\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1072 #uuid "647415db-3b65-5b2e-8b3d-f70381af49e3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357466 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357316 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1069 "q-90688" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1072 #uuid "51ab68ae-6386-58f8-941d-fb2c589605a8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357467 1074 "Using the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{({v}^{2}+1){x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (v+\\frac{1}{v})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({v}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) & =C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357301 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1069 "q-90691" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1072 #uuid "2b4ed6f3-beb2-5be5-83cb-e8d41e7b8f3b" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357468 1074 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{3})}{{v}^{2}{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356915 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1069 "q-90692" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{x}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1072 #uuid "ed854c8a-5a47-5533-b48c-04deb7b81625" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357469 1074 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+2v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}|x|) \\\\ |v+1| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}|x| \\\\ v+1 & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}x \\\\ v+1 & =Cx \\\\ v & =Cx-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =Cx-1 \\\\ y & =C{x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357107 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1069 "q-90693" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1072 #uuid "73b06f86-eb7f-5333-916f-45bb6952f791" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357470 1074 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357237 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1069 "q-90694" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1071 "Since $2005$, the population of a certain town grows at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n \n\n$$\nr(t)=200{e}^{0.2t}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years since $2005.$\n\nTo the nearest integer, by how many people did the population of the town increase between the years $2010$ and $2015?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1072 #uuid "4b115e56-9fa6-5d07-94d1-e0046f6cdbd9" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357471 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** in population $r(t).$ To find the change in  population between  $2010$ and $2015$, we integrate $r(t)$ between the$t=5$ and $t=10.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{10}200{e}^{0.2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =200\\cdot {{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,{{e}^{0.2t}|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =1\\,000({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & ≈4\\,671\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.  \n\nTherefore, between  $2010$ and $2015$ the population of the town increased by $4\\,671$ people." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357340 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1069 "q-90699" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1072 #uuid "bd483d10-bcb0-5d3a-aec6-2a397c70d709" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357472 1074 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357164 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1069 "q-90705" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-24y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1072 #uuid "4cbc6a7d-b508-53fb-a60e-7e0a5e087bd9" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357473 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =6.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357271 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1069 "q-90709" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-14y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1072 #uuid "2680a0da-7f34-50eb-834d-2854fcbc4646" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357474 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-7$ or $\\lambda =2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357211 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1069 "q-90719" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+15y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1072 #uuid "53fbca9b-a9e4-5e0a-8181-574662143d68" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357475 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =5.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357172 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1069 "q-90720" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1072 #uuid "cb0ac50b-6a2d-5d53-997b-cb65e0ad2bb8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357476 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356997 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1069 "q-90722" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-11\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+28y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1072 #uuid "d5c18e84-2e36-57eb-8c9a-1a101f9f859c" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357477 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-11\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+28{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +28=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357078 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1069 "q-90727" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1072 #uuid "b6ebbe36-257b-53a0-8f7f-d2ce63fbc0f6" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357478 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{x}$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356917 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1069 "q-90729" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+24y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1072 #uuid "937412e4-d54f-5aaa-9272-a7d19f395a07" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357479 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357284 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1069 "q-90756" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1071 "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1072 #uuid "2f28ae95-03b9-593b-a6e6-a24580ae5aa5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357480 1074 "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357124 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1069 "q-90758" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1071 "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1072 #uuid "0121e26f-4108-56d0-ac26-f4c71b23dab4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357481 1074 "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356921 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1069 "q-90763" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1072 #uuid "25f23664-d443-5442-98c5-f5ec1e9dae76" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357482 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357183 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1069 "q-90779" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1071 "A paddling pool contains $6$ gallons of water initially. At $t=0,$ water is pumped into the pool at a constant rate of $3$ gallons per minute. After $t=2$ minutes, water starts leaking from the pool at a rate of $\\sqrt{t-2}$ gallons per minute. How many gallons of water are in the pool at time $t$, for $t\\ge 2?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1072 #uuid "77acaf25-d2cb-5dc5-89b1-40927de25895" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357483 1074 "Let $f(t)=3-\\sqrt{t-2}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing for $t\\ge 2.$ Note that we subtract $\\sqrt{t-2}$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $F(T)$ be the volume of the pool after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T)=F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the pool contains $6+2\\cdot 3=12$ gallons of water. Substituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{2}^{T}(3-\\sqrt{t-2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{(3t-\\frac{2}{3}{(t-2)}^{3/2})|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =12+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}-(3(2)-\\frac{2}{3}(2-2)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =6+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of our original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357244 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1069 "q-90791" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=7.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1072 #uuid "0bc0c8a7-8858-5e00-ae94-4f1aa0312ddd" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357484 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+49=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=7.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7P\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=7$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n7Q=7.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357029 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1069 "q-90793" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+100y=0,\\,y(0)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=20.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1072 #uuid "9afdbaee-36d2-5005-b5ca-9e82e70cfccf" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357485 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=5.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=20.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-10P\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+10Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=20$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n10Q=20.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=5$ and $Q=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357170 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1069 "q-90797" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1072 #uuid "b2207d8d-96f3-5382-94c1-3544f222beb5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357486 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356950 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1069 "q-90799" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+3y=0,$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1072 #uuid "6abf6154-8a42-5d1d-a162-51b8be1696db" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357487 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357090 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1069 "q-90802" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1072 #uuid "1a885511-1dcf-5606-836f-b33f26907c6e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357488 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357013 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1069 "q-90806" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1072 #uuid "2c7ec34b-d26e-57cf-bfab-3aae321fbf59" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357489 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-2+4\\text{i})x})={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-2+4\\text{i})x})={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357075 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1069 "q-90807" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1072 #uuid "1d14f94c-eee0-5194-aa26-bfd57687e00b" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357490 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357251 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1069 "q-9084" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1071 "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1072 #uuid "bc51d516-c7eb-513e-b5c1-77d70dc84dc8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357491 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357290 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1069 "q-90844" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1072 #uuid "0300bce9-a20a-5c73-afd7-2cf9d44f40db" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357492 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,z(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,dx+x\\,dy+dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{z}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{z}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356956 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1069 "q-90848" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1072 #uuid "d77bf889-9285-57c2-9d70-8b359a57d0fb" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357493 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357005 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1069 "q-90871" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1072 #uuid "067b683a-f3df-5f66-b795-e612d7f9ac62" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357494 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357136 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1069 "q-9091" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1071 "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1072 #uuid "979e3e94-fbe0-52cd-a657-896495d69070" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357495 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356991 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1069 "q-90911" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1072 #uuid "8c4c99c3-e164-55a4-8452-aa0c671a6241" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357496 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357093 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1069 "q-90957" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+18\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+81y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1072 #uuid "70c6ea26-9f95-5abe-a439-1776801fcf90" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357497 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+18\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+81{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+18\\lambda +81) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+18\\lambda +81=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +9)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-9$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-9x}+Bx{e}^{-9x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357205 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1069 "q-90958" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-14\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+49y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1072 #uuid "ee8781b5-1cb6-5737-93a0-098c0c971d2f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357498 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)(\\lambda -7) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+Bx{e}^{7x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357216 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1069 "q-90964" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1072 #uuid "18e84e12-ab22-5aef-8e4e-67807fdf41a5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357499 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x/2}$ and $x{e}^{-3x/2}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $x{e}^{-3x/2}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x/2}$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357015 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1069 "q-90965" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1072 #uuid "09b620a1-f5a6-59df-bc00-195150ac5c28" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357500 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and $x{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-3x}+3x$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357059 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1069 "q-90966" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-16{y}^{′}+64y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1072 #uuid "ced6ca5a-46ff-55d4-9045-c405e7b10434" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357501 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-16\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+64{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -8)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =8$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{8x}$ and $x{e}^{8x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${x}^{2}{e}^{8x}$ is not a solution." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357201 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1069 "q-91033" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1071 "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1072 #uuid "cad3f9f6-5f4f-5dd6-a4bd-e43304af3066" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357502 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=-2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357227 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1069 "q-91035" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1071 "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1072 #uuid "d7bb92a9-d970-5cb7-a354-b33662cccbde" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357503 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1)+x(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})+x(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =-2+2+x \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357088 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1069 "q-91043" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1071 "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1072 #uuid "5db0460a-3d42-521d-a875-a7a59116452a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357504 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{3}=1,{a}_{2}=-3,{a}_{1}=4,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357104 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1069 "q-91045" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1071 "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{4}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1072 #uuid "28f79904-dc76-5fc8-83e0-f5f1318fada1" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357505 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}={x}^{2},{f}_{2}=-2x,{f}_{1}=x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}){x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{4})-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{4})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(4{x}^{3})-2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{3})+x(4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2})-2x(12{x}^{2})+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(24x)-24{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =24{x}^{3}-24{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357241 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1069 "q-91158" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 21\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1072 #uuid "be625aba-1e4a-556c-a78e-3cfc2c1b0666" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357506 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{22}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357163 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1069 "q-91160" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1072 #uuid "5b61d5ca-7f09-5da1-b160-0e00fef59814" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357507 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357108 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1069 "q-91162" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1072 #uuid "ee46ab50-1172-582c-9154-5604520f146c" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357508 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{31}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357192 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1069 "q-91169" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1072 #uuid "04c4a850-58dd-5ed4-a8bf-edb5b51c508e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357509 1074 "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\