equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-4){C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357109 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1069 "q-91221" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1072 #uuid "9a551105-1281-563b-9139-cdf718553556" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357519 1074 "Let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-4){C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357200 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1069 "q-91229" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1071 "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1072 #uuid "9f5a627e-5fd5-5321-b04e-df13a3c56af4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357520 1074 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-6-5) \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357084 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1069 "q-91232" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1071 "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1072 #uuid "15878489-f600-5d8d-a45e-e1a6680dcacf" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357521 1074 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(49-25) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357012 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1069 "q-91322" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1071 "![](images/82/82bdf962acd0e4a9beb29c7f3cc4930e66e6e56f41f81ba944fd470edfdb2cd8.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1072 #uuid "039700b4-f4c5-56d4-8abe-64b6a995ab04" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357522 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec38ac9d02fbc8ec8c4f79fccb36a0e91833e852127fd1a8c84e492276b8f95d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356998 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1069 "q-91439" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1071 "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\text{cm/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1072 #uuid "bf7169db-886c-5e0f-937a-7a6a8c57466a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357523 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](images/f9/f97581877814a2626d820b07b4e7a2c3d637f9369b119d0e0c3896c77e15f964.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357254 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1069 "q-91537" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1071 "A man accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of his motion. He then jogs at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for another $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1072 #uuid "3a0ad72a-0584-5c09-9aad-8767338eae10" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357524 1074 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the man starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $0.3.$ \n\nHowever, because the man is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the man's speed has increased by $0.3\\cdot 10=3\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,3).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a6d3b2d3f1664aed35ea11b064f6f75a3707a2051414ca9015a690148f06c16.png)\n\nThe man then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=10+20=30.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b579d714470aa484c23447647864b165c5b453e56e56262ace4c6d5d6b7d5f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=30$, the man has an acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.1.$ The third line segment stops after $20$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(30,3)$ and has a slope of $0.1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1(t-30) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1t-3 \\\\ v & =0.1t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=30+20=50$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.1t \\\\ v & =0.1(50) \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(50,5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](images/bd/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png)" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357305 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1069 "q-91547" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1071 "A school bus accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $30\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $2$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours before decelerating with a constant deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ until it stops. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1072 #uuid "b913363e-49f4-5f7d-a18d-2fac843687bc" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357525 1074 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the school bus starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $30\\,{\\text{km/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $30.$ \n\nHowever, because the school bus is only accelerating for the first $2$ hours, we stop the line at $t=2.$ At this time, the school bus's speed has increased by $2\\cdot 30=60\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(2,60).$\n\n![](images/21/2126d713b00d66abb0d06e5878fdadc543c9c8a3a0000eb2f56b1048a2824ca4.png)\n\nThe school bus then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours. So, the speed remains at $60\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $2$ hours, until $t=2+2=4.$\n\n![](images/65/6512a9d80ca9de16f5384031590b7ae5e58a9a2871bd5ac7aace1803f0d82603.png)\n\nAfter $t=4$, the school bus has a deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $-20.$ The third line segment stops when the school bus's speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(4,60)$ and has a slope of $-20,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-60 & =-20(t-4) \\\\ v-60 & =-20t+80 \\\\ v & =-20t+140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the school bus is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-20t+140 \\\\ 0 & =-20t+140 \\\\ 20t & =140 \\\\ t & =\\frac{140}{20} \\\\ t & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(7,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](images/23/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png)" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357215 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1069 "q-91549" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1071 "A train accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then the train decelerates with a constant deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the next $15$ seconds, before traveling at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1072 #uuid "cf428e0b-b18d-5488-b85c-dbf731bbdf40" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357526 1074 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the train starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $3.$ \n\nHowever, because the train is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the train's speed has increased by $10\\cdot 3=30\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,30).$\n\n![](images/54/54ac34c5fe7833d42fb8ddda729d3823ff0e726e1e6228492639a9e91bad2762.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the train has a deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the second line segment is $-1.$ The second line segment stops after $15$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10,30)$ and has a slope of $-1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-30 & =-(t-10) \\\\ v-30 & =-t+10 \\\\ v & =-t+40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second line segment stops when $t=10+15=25$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-t+40 \\\\ v & =-25+40 \\\\ v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b1/b199170cfdadd688a15668afdbac796a90fc158c94bfc0ffc8b9245c5a741e8e.png)\n\nThe train then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $15\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=25+20=45.$\n\nFinally, the third line segment stops at the point $(45,15).$ The final graph is\n\n![](images/b1/b1ece8dd03146aaf420746b6ba3cc59b3d707150fe3682bbdcd6f05d645fb4c4.png)" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357071 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1069 "q-9253" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1072 #uuid "0704d357-8cb1-56bb-b120-20ad34cf98ff" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357527 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356981 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1069 "q-9255" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1072 #uuid "da6f4133-24dc-5140-aaa0-0ea075dfe3f5" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357528 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357253 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1069 "q-9258" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1072 #uuid "1174578d-6631-5fcf-81bf-729c1fac3da6" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357529 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357062 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357530 1069 "q-9260" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186