0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357008 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1069 "q-9261" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,2),(-5,4),(-3,2),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(2,0)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1072 #uuid "a755a352-feaa-5539-8e01-5e0ad683e5ae" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357531 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0),(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356972 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1069 "q-9262" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1072 #uuid "c32a27cb-e2ec-5eaa-a1cb-1ea5655b2b83" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357532 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357289 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1069 "q-9263" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1072 #uuid "4a6a4708-c258-5b5f-8c4c-fa774844e5ba" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357533 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357114 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1069 "q-9264" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-2,0)$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1072 #uuid "56746454-856c-5001-bcf8-93772c0debce" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357534 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357169 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1069 "q-92849" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1071 "A rock band has $48$ songs, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of which are ballads. How many of the band's songs are not ballads?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1072 #uuid "9b54539a-5b7b-5197-b835-ec723294fe26" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357535 1074 "We are given that the ratio of ballads to the total number of songs is $\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of ballads, and the denominator represents the total number of songs .\n\nWe can make a denominator of $48$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $12:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 12}{4\\times 12}=\\frac{12}{48}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the band has $12$ ballads. \n\nSo the number of songs that are not ballads is\n\n$$\n48-12=36.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357222 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1069 "q-92925" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1071 "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={3}^{-3x+1}-3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1072 #uuid "85f8aaba-752e-5d0d-a0e1-df489c3df796" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357536 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{-3(0)+1}-3 \\\\  & ={3}^{1}-3 \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357196 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1069 "q-92929" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1071 "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2x}+1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1072 #uuid "30982409-0430-5b49-824a-6fc7b9e1ff5a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357537 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2(0)}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357018 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1069 "q-92930" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1071 "What is the $y$-intercept of $y=-5{e}^{-{x}^{3}}+3?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1072 #uuid "ab1d8b5d-ee01-5c7e-86ea-f76938391b95" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357538 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5{e}^{-(0)^{3}}+3 \\\\  & =-5{e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =-5(1)+3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357157 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1069 "q-92959" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=2({3}^{x-5})-3.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ then $a=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1072 #uuid "d7194b7d-6fd2-51a3-9281-bf2168470395" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357539 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2c3036fb3f8c92ab09067fca4117e6fead9026e2fd926e174fefbe9037cbcf.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-3$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -3\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357239 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1069 "q-9296" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1071 "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1072 #uuid "172d8100-18cc-569e-9d95-d342ebc00693" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357540 1074 "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,2\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357295 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1069 "q-92963" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x-3}+2.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1072 #uuid "e35f675f-b059-5f5e-9a19-8ac6f6c3e6e7" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357541 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/86/8646efab1e707e57bd8ff93b5f3f7b1336ae23588abf2d423a45bbcf702f86ab.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357137 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1069 "q-92987" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1071 "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)={5}^{2x-3}+2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1072 #uuid "9a05ddeb-e8ca-5093-80cf-5472fc6b0d04" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357542 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/88/88b60a3595314e2d14816b74b9c2b37115de53785548a0a7023f6f27dd8e55b8.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356964 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1069 "q-92988" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1071 "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=2({e}^{2x})-5.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1072 #uuid "43f0b9fb-d57d-5094-ba73-d0cc6a85c5c3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357543 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc2af8bae74f163f0dd4eae74fd90fc6c5460cae552c7e5072c83521444aea29.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-5$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356973 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1069 "q-92995" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{4}.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1072 #uuid "1738a08a-0e6c-5ca9-b9ee-772359397eb8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357544 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-{e}^{4}=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${e}^{4}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-\\cancel{{e}^{4}}+\\cancel{{e}^{4}} & =0+{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $e$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357079 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1069 "q-92997" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=5(7)^{x}-35.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1072 #uuid "01084d42-8456-5e14-a7fe-d4c887a9b38c" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357545 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n5(7)^{x}-35=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $35$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(7)^{x}-35 & =0 \\\\ 5(7)^{x}-\\cancel{35}+\\cancel{35} & =0+35 \\\\ 5(7)^{x} & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(7)^{x} & =35 \\\\ {7}^{x} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(7)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357130 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1069 "q-92998" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=4{e}^{2x-1}-8.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1072 #uuid "dc356c61-c382-5786-b505-51f9c59fb0a9" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357546 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x-1}-8=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1}-8 & =0 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1}-\\cancel{8}+\\cancel{8} & =0+8 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1} & =8 \\\\ {e}^{2x-1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357039 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1069 "q-93000" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1072 #uuid "24488487-89b2-5c0e-9650-62ff06ab7b9a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357547 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $10$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+\\cancel{10}-\\cancel{10} & =0-10 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $-15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{10}{15} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $\\frac{2}{3}$ as ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(\\frac{3}{2})$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =-1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=0.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357118 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1069 "q-9309" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1071 "![](images/bd/bd50e99a129b1d6a2c6b73145dbf68681bc92a3cef0ca8e743b63cf96c9afafc.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1072 #uuid "59864426-00bd-5d3f-88fd-eb20e5c838df" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357548 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4)=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-1)=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,11\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356920 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1069 "q-93098" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=-5\\sqrt[4]{3x+1}+10.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1072 #uuid "0afad6e9-b050-52e7-9a98-3c68d7beb393" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357549 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-5\\sqrt[4]{3x+1}+10=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\sqrt[4]{3x+1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -5\\sqrt[4]{3x+1} & =-10 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{3x+1} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt[4]{3x+1})^{4} & ={2}^{4} \\\\ 3x+1 & =16 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\sqrt[4]{3\\cdot 5+1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -5\\sqrt[4]{16}+10 & =0 \\\\ -5\\cdot 2+10 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=5.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356933 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1069 "q-9311" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1071 "![](images/24/24eadcb5300028866834aec17b105794078a17606085191315475a9aae3be5ef.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1072 #uuid "2b5f0a39-7236-5bad-a73d-49918e9dc1b0" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357550 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=7\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-1)=19.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,19\\,|\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356948 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1069 "q-93262" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1071 "![](images/d2/d2fe4346f503215ec6594655cf633ad46b62bc87572339bd1728e6535bef851a.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the rectangular pyramid represented by the net above?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1072 #uuid "a6e9a8a2-4b94-511f-b227-3b7ec05e7b0a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357551 1074 "The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 150+4\\times 80 \\\\  & =150+320 \\\\  & =470\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357191 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1069 "q-93339" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1071 "![](images/a6/a60731e7df54bb8f18f834b818c3e49c79fb30e67f7ed50c40f40aa813689ece.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces an unfair tetrahedral die from a plastic net, shown above. What is the total surface area of the die?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1072 #uuid "b7e0efa5-6423-5a61-a04b-01649c479f66" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357552 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ base triangle and $3$ side triangles. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of base})+3\\times (\\text{Area of side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 64+3\\times 78 \\\\  & =64+234 \\\\  & =298\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357186 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1069 "q-93344" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1071 "![](images/76/76e1fb6d652758a0e20877d7cacbd1172a0073d2ada557d1761ab6e59aab06d9.png)\n\nA gardening company is making metal spikes from the net shown above. What is the total surface area of the spike?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1072 #uuid "456ec2db-1c88-58ae-ba2f-bbea66829bda" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357553 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a rectangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 12+4\\times 6 \\\\  & =12+24 \\\\  & =36\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357003 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1069 "q-93345" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1071 "![](images/a8/a8264c635006c74c2d9a7ca4eb3ab8d937ae7b270b4567373d022186c787bbc1.png)\n\nAn artist makes a glass sculpture. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the sculpture. What is the total surface area of the sculpture?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1072 #uuid "8310ff8f-6f97-5b00-a4a6-70a7ec67c1ad" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357554 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular prism. The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+3\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & =2\\times 8+3\\times 17 \\\\  & =16+51 \\\\  & =67\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357033 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1069 "q-9340" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1072 #uuid "7bbc0e33-82aa-5775-853a-c2bdd5e62b0b" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357555 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-7\\cdot 7 & =0 \\\\ 4-2\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 45}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 14}{2} \\\\  & =-5,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =9.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357328 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1069 "q-9348" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1072 #uuid "b9a0d751-b8e6-598d-8c36-71f74a16fdfb" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357556 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -2+\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357204 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1069 "q-93668" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=20\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1072 #uuid "cefd1b85-dc14-5b21-adc0-70bc4c017c61" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357557 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+5\\alpha =20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357141 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1069 "q-93669" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=4.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1072 #uuid "3ffa1972-44ba-58bc-8c72-dd68f1c5677a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357558 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$