ives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{0}+2(0){e}^{0}+2{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =-3A+B+2 \\\\ 0 & =-3A+B \\\\ B & =3A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=4 \\\\ B=3A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=3.$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=3,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357046 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1069 "q-93715" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1071 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1072 #uuid "95ad491b-4ee0-5e7b-8a0c-33d2b7cf1e9a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357567 1074 "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357247 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1069 "q-93721" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1071 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1072 #uuid "d6e8b60c-c6c9-5753-b254-94e3e9635292" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357568 1074 "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357184 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1069 "q-93778" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1071 "For the differential equation $x{y}^{″}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=1,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1072 #uuid "82f3b471-1300-53ec-a6ab-1f87df3079fd" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357569 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357214 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1069 "q-93779" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1071 "For the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=16,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1072 #uuid "976177b6-9509-59d5-9332-759ff3c36f2f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357570 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357030 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1069 "q-9378" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1072 #uuid "1d5b8de5-8041-5436-aa39-27f99669da1c" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357571 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356995 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1069 "q-93781" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1071 "For the differential equation $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=2{x}^{3},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1072 #uuid "95040ada-8589-5447-afcd-eeb64a65cf69" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357572 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356927 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1069 "q-9379" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1072 #uuid "ca1d168a-973f-5cf3-be95-af0c79523ae0" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357573 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357025 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1069 "q-93811" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=18x+3.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1072 #uuid "431e20fe-7075-5147-8313-e3b2fa443237" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357574 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357252 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1069 "q-93812" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=8x-32.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1072 #uuid "8c30a937-0ea3-5db8-9128-f664cf20cbb6" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357575 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-32\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-16\\alpha x-16\\beta =8x-32.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-16\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357188 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1069 "q-93815" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1072 #uuid "d5db3819-3896-5dd1-927d-34f395bd5bf0" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357576 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+16{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\la