linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357582 1072 #uuid "96f1b97e-7350-56fc-a13d-baf13d00782b" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357582 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357101 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1069 "q-93830" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=24{x}^{2}+4x+2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1072 #uuid "f5ac94c5-db29-5df8-9837-9dccebfd6c41" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357583 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +7(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(14\\alpha +12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 14\\alpha +12\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357347 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1069 "q-93833" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1072 #uuid "50d3f5fe-3c9a-51a8-8637-85b029f95cf4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357584 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B(0){e}^{-2(0)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(0) \\\\ -1 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-2B(0){e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(0) \\\\ -2 & =-2A+B \\\\ -2 & =-2+B \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=0,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356999 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1069 "q-93834" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1072 #uuid "b7a88e30-518f-56b9-b0cb-be9508a05d98" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357585 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{2(0)}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(0) \\\\ 3 & =A+B-1 \\\\ 4 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+2B{e}^{2x}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+2B{e}^{2(0)}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(0) \\\\ 0 & =A+2B-6 \\\\ 6 & =A+2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=4 \\\\ A+2B=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=2.$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=2,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356943 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1069 "q-93840" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1072 #uuid "6b88dc5d-f9bd-5d35-9a4c-c9d2e58e7fc3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357586 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}-2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+B{e}^{x}-4{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}-4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A+B-4 \\\\ 0 & =2+B-4 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=2,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357354 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1069 "q-93844" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1072 #uuid "d556036d-1452-5095-a217-ddbd9e1d3782" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357587 1074 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}y′′+bty′+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-t{y}^{′}+6y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356961 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1069 "q-93847" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1072 #uuid "08f0c812-628e-5391-b195-304393251976" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357588 1074 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}y′′+bty′+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-2y=t+1.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356993 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1069 "q-93849" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1072 #uuid "3b82f0a3-2342-5767-8e48-79c6839efa78" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357589 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(4\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357119 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1069 "q-9385" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1072 #uuid "0000fc83-77f5-5913-a1b9-b3ae53df5b9e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357590 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356916 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1069 "q-93850" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1072 #uuid "f3f4b670-1379-5c73-adb0-10e911f81586" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357591 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+7x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(2\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356969 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1069 "q-93855" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{x},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1072 #uuid "294b5818-ba13-502f-b91f-002cd00c35f4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357592 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1-{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{3{c}_{1}+1}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =3\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357332 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1069 "q-93856" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1072 #uuid "851aedbb-5eb3-501d-8f6e-9a49fc82d0b4" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357593 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=1-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}\\sqrt{t}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =1-{c}_{2}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{c}_{2}+2}{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =6\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357113 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1069 "q-93858" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}x,\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1072 #uuid "a4d26131-9a53-50ca-b1a0-12e882a821a6" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357594 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(2)=1.$ Substituting $y(2)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{4}+2{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=2.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{{x}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=2$ and ${c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-\\frac{8-16{c}_{2}}{8}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & =3{c}_{2}-1 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356946 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1069 "q-93862" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{1/3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1072 #uuid "f07b7590-9dbd-5779-adf7-64162225dffb" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357595 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{x}^{2/3}}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5{c}_{1}}{6} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357327 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1069 "q-93885" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1072 #uuid "7184253c-12f9-5a04-bfb4-0a1a49365dfe" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357596 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =-5 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)), \\\\ {x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}+B{x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =Ax(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+Bx(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =(A+B)x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as \n\n$$\ny(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356980 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1069 "q-93886" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+7y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1072 #uuid "c3671b96-1486-5d07-837c-088ca2ef4945" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357597 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +7] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =-3 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={t}^{2}{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={t}^{2}{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as \n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357166 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1069 "q-93894" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-5,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1072 #uuid "9a74ba80-7c8d-5b7e-9914-e60d3b4687e1" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357598 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=-5$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & ={c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357126 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357599 1069 "q-93895" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [175921863