$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357609 1072 #uuid "e96ec148-dcd3-5736-b705-6d0e6370e151" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357609 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 8+1\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =18-8-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357336 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1069 "q-93971" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1071 "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}].$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1072 #uuid "5ab765c1-eee5-5779-8d72-46166b59e774" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357610 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 6+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-20+18+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357304 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1069 "q-93976" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1072 #uuid "85015656-8453-52b3-9c85-0ce7686e258d" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357611 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357041 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1069 "q-93977" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,-2 & 4 \\\\ \\,2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1072 #uuid "0f0bce3e-c7ad-5cbe-a214-649d56d182b8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357612 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357223 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1069 "q-93981" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1071 "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)=2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1072 #uuid "9dfbbfd5-5653-5197-9e53-90e5b57031e3" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357613 1074 "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-24-1-0 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357117 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1069 "q-93982" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1072 #uuid "77aee134-9112-5914-aa23-0b453cd37f3f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357614 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357103 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1069 "q-93983" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1072 #uuid "43095d6b-d4b1-5b1d-ad8b-056e30c008dd" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357615 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356996 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1069 "q-93984" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1072 #uuid "09763352-7b89-5e95-8516-57e6bce8d181" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357616 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357333 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1069 "q-93985" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1072 #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357617 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357287 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1069 "q-93988" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1072 #uuid "cc49e39d-dc41-5d92-84ab-b1310e571077" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357618 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356992 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1069 "q-93993" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1072 #uuid "0a5f4b21-f8de-5f12-8b8c-c4be1021742e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357619 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357156 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1069 "q-93995" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1072 #uuid "45479840-98ff-5a0a-8836-361250ff785d" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357620 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357233 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1069 "q-93996" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1072 #uuid "9eb1f202-37a9-5513-9728-465e7c426c0d" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357621 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357050 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1069 "q-93999" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1072 #uuid "f0da0513-2306-5296-a63a-c968889ba8e8" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357622 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357110 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1069 "q-94005" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1072 #uuid "22ccae12-46d9-5422-8c88-61d112d8d942" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357623 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357153 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1069 "q-94006" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+15y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1072 #uuid "54ccfb65-7e54-5949-bba7-09339d2681aa" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357624 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+5B{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n3A+5B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ 3A+5B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=4,B=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{e}^{3x}-2{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357097 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1069 "q-94007" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+8y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(0)=9,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=24.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1072 #uuid "a1ea3f1a-b518-5ab9-b9f1-47877b73191f" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357625 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=9$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=9.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=24,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+4B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=24$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A+4B & =24 \\\\ A+2B & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=9 \\\\ A+2B=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=6,B=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357181 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1069 "q-94008" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1072 #uuid "3f4314ce-c016-5a5b-a906-45de8df0762e" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357626 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357356 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1069 "q-94009" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{5}{13} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1072 #uuid "6a90b3ef-f0f0-5198-bb72-8d712e554612" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357627 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357298 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1069 "q-94011" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+10y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-3.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1072 #uuid "16b6daa1-1a79-55ed-98ca-cc102191facb" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357628 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-3,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}-2B{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-3$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-5A-2B=-3.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -5A-2B=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3,B=-6.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357334 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1069 "q-94017" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(0)=8,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1072 #uuid "996ecbd7-d457-5eef-ace4-2ef3cc5c9746" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357629 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=8.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-x}-Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+B=0.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=8$ gives $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=8{e}^{-x}+8x{e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357283 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1069 "q-94020" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1072 #uuid "169defb3-7918-5f63-aa50-008ef745a1e0" 13194139533432 17592186045585 true] [17592186357630 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n